# Atlas Decision Register

Append-only. A decision is added here, never edited after acceptance — a
reversal is a new decision that references the one it retires. Execution
Plan Technical Lane step 1 requires this file exist in the repo alongside
the methodology, operating system and execution plan.

Per Methodology §10 (Governing Rule): "No methodology version becomes
client-active from convenience, provider deprecation or software limitation
alone. The decision is documented first, then implemented, then applied
forward." Decisions are documented before implementation; any exception is
noted in the row itself.

| ID | Date | Decision | Status |
|---|---|---|---|
| D-001 | pre-v1.0 | Dual-score AVI-composite + AVS model | **Retired** by D-028 |
| D-028 | 2026-08-25 | Retire AVI before Client #1. Atlas will not combine an observed outcome and controllable levers into one headline composite. Official client metrics are AVS (outcome) and Atlas Readiness Score (controllable), reported separately. Historical compatibility with AVI is unnecessary because no paying client baseline had yet been issued. | Active |
| D-029 | 2026-08-26 | Gemini adapter authenticates via Vertex AI (service account / Application Default Credentials, standard Cloud Billing) instead of an AI Studio authorization key. Reason: AI Studio's prepay-credits billing UI hit an unresolvable bug blocking G0 closure. Forced consequence, not a separate choice: the Interactions API is Gemini Developer API only and is not available on Vertex AI at time of writing, so the adapter's grounding call was also rewritten from `client.interactions.create` to `client.models.generate_content` with the `google_search` tool — the standard endpoint Vertex AI does support today. The grounding contract (grounded vs excluded, retry-once-with-explicit-instruction, Methodology §8.1) is unchanged; only the API surface implementing it changed. Grounding unit cost in `config.py` is carried forward provisionally and flagged for reconfirmation against the live Vertex AI billing export. | Active |
| D-030 | 2026-08-26 | Gemini model pin dropped from `gemini-3.7-flash` to `gemini-2.5-flash` for the Vertex AI path opened by D-029. Reason: first live run against the real Vertex AI endpoint returned a clean 404 NOT_FOUND for `gemini-3.7-flash` ("Publisher model ... was not found or your project does not have access to it") — the Gemini 3.x line is not yet GA on Vertex AI, confirmed as a model-availability gap and not a permissions issue (a separate 403 IAM gap was hit and fixed first; the 404 is a distinct, later error). `gemini-2.5-flash` was live-tested successfully against the same project/location before being adopted. `PROVIDER_PRICING["gemini"]` in `config.py` updated to Vertex AI's confirmed gemini-2.5-flash rates ($0.30/$2.50 per MTok, $35/1,000 grounded prompts beyond a 1,500/day free quota) — materially different from the AI Studio rates D-029 had carried forward, confirming those needed independent reconfirmation rather than being assumed interchangeable. Revisit both the model pin and its pricing once Gemini 3.x reaches Vertex AI GA. | Active |
| D-031 | 2026-08-26 | Perplexity adapter (Technical Lane step 6) built against the classic Sonar Chat Completions API (`model="sonar"`, `api.perplexity.ai/chat/completions`), not Perplexity's newer Agent API (`client.responses.create`). Reason: Perplexity confirmed Sonar Chat Completions stays available until September 27, 2026 — about a month of runway from build date — while the Agent API's default model routing (its docs reference `openai/gpt-5.1` as a default and third-party models like `openai/gpt-5.6-luna` as preset options), exact Perplexity-native model identifier, and full response/citation shape could not be confirmed with enough certainty during research to build against safely, unlike the well-documented, stable Sonar Chat Completions surface. Operating System §5 already names Sonar specifically, which this decision follows. Consequence: this adapter has a hard rebuild deadline of 2026-09-27, tracked here, regardless of which replacement (Agent API or otherwise) is chosen — do not let it lapse silently past that date. | Active |
| D-032 | 2026-08-26 | `evidence.observation_id` and `costs.observation_id` given a unique constraint (migration 0003), and the new `atlas.runners.resume` writer upserts both tables on that conflict key. Reason: Technical Lane step 8 (retry/idempotency/reconciliation for the observation pipeline) requires a resumed/retried run to never double-write the evidence or cost ledger for a task it already finished. A "SELECT then INSERT if missing" application-level check is a check-then-act race whenever two runner invocations can overlap (e.g. a manual Recovery Dispatch run against a run_plan a scheduled run is also touching) — hit while designing the idempotency guarantee itself, not a hypothetical. The unique constraint plus on_conflict upsert closes that race at the database level instead of trusting apart-in-time application checks. | Active |
| D-033 | 2026-08-26 | Accepted residual risk, documented rather than solved: if a runner process dies after a provider call succeeds but before the observation row's finalizing UPDATE commits, the task is left in `running`, gets requeued to `queued` by the next reconciliation pass (Operating System §4), and `atlas.runners.resume` will call that provider a second time for the same task on the next attempt. None of the four providers wired so far (OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini/Vertex, Perplexity) expose a documented client-supplied idempotency key for their generation endpoints, so this window cannot be closed without one of: (a) a provider adding native request idempotency, or (b) a durable, atomic "provider call receipt" persisted between the network call returning and this process reading it back, which no component in this pipeline currently provides. D-032's unique-constraint + upsert still guarantees the evidence/cost *ledger* never double-writes even when the provider is redialed — this decision only accepts that the redial itself (and its cost) can happen. Revisit if a provider ships call-level idempotency keys, or if double-billed provider calls are observed often enough in production to justify building a receipt layer. | Active |
| D-034 | 2026-08-27 | Scoring engine input boundary is the `recommendations` table, confirmed before any scoring code was written. The engine reads rows whose `rpv`, `rank`, `outcome_type`, `is_client_entity` and `entity_conflict` are already populated and aggregates them per Methodology §4.3; it never reads `observations.raw_response` or `Outcome.parsed_recommendations`. How an RPV was assigned — a human today, a parser later once it clears Execution Plan §10's ">=95% on labelled recommendation/rank/entity sample before unattended scoring" gate — is deliberately invisible to scoring, so the parser can be swapped in later without the scoring math changing. Forced by the state of the pipeline at build time: `recommendations` held zero rows in the live project and no code path writes it (all four adapters construct `Outcome(raw_response=...)` and leave `parsed_recommendations` empty by explicit design note), so there is no automated raw-observation-to-RPV path, and Technical Lane step 9 must not create one. Load-bearing consequence: a complete observation with **no** recommendation row is "not yet parsed", counts against §6.2 observation completeness, and is never read as RPV 0.00 — scoring a zero requires an explicit row carrying `absent`, `source_only_mention` or `negative_mention`, which are tracked distinctly and never collapsed into each other. `entity_conflict = true` rows are excluded from AVS entirely (§4.1) and retained for P3. | Active |
| D-035 | 2026-08-27 | `recommendations.outcome_type` vocabulary aligned to Methodology §4.1 by migration 0004: the check constraint's `source_only` becomes `source_only_mention` and `negative` becomes `negative_mention`. Reason: migration 0001's check constraint and the `OutcomeType` enum in `atlas/adapters/base.py` disagreed on two of six values, and D-034 makes that table the scoring engine's only input — a loader cannot read `outcome_type` without one canonical vocabulary. Resolved toward the methodology's own names (§4.1 names the trackers SOURCE_ONLY_MENTION and NEGATIVE_MENTION explicitly) rather than translating in the loader, because a silent mapping layer at the boundary is the kind of hidden semantics D-034 exists to prevent. Safe as a constraint swap with no data migration: the live `recommendations` table was verified empty (0 rows) before this was written. Migration 0004 is **not yet applied** to the live project and must be applied before the first manual RPV row is entered; until then the loader raises on a legacy value and names this migration rather than silently accepting it. | Active |
| D-036 | 2026-08-27 | The set of eligible platforms is a required explicit argument to AVS computation, with no default and no inference from the observations present. Reason: Methodology §4.3 defines AVS as the mean PlatformScore "across eligible calibrated platforms", and §8.4 makes eligibility the *output* of a calibration gate (raw mention agreement >=80% and Cohen kappa >=0.60, or >=85% plus documented manual review) that has not been run — it is roadmap Week 5 — and no schema column anywhere records per-platform eligibility. Defaulting to "every platform present in the data" would silently fold uncalibrated, evidence-only platforms into a client-facing AVS, which §8.4 forbids ("A platform failing the gate remains evidence-only and is excluded from AVS"). Requiring the caller to name the eligible set makes that a deliberate, auditable act. The engine additionally refuses to score a platform named eligible for which it holds no scoreable observation, rather than averaging over a missing term. | Active |
| D-037 | 2026-08-27 | The §6.2 hierarchical paired bootstrap is implemented on the standard library's `random.Random` (Mersenne Twister) with a caller-supplied seed that is required, not optional, and is returned with the result for storage alongside the score row. No numpy, scipy or statsmodels dependency is added. Reason: a client-facing interval must be exactly reproducible from stored inputs (§9 requires every score row carry its interval bounds and evidence references; §8.4 freezes v1.0 only once "the score engine reproduces hand calculations"). `random.Random` is documented as reproducible across Python versions for a given seed, whereas numpy's `Generator` stream is explicitly not guaranteed stable across releases. Performance was measured rather than assumed and does not force a tradeoff: 10,000 resamples over 40 prompts x 4 platforms x 5 replicates completes in 1.6 s in pure Python (3.3 s at 80 prompts). The 2.5th/97.5th percentiles use linear interpolation between closest ranks, verified against `numpy.percentile(..., method="linear")` to 7.1e-15 over 200 random arrays x 5 quantiles. The Wilson interval used for Mention Rate and Top-3 Rate is likewise hand-implemented, verified against `statsmodels.stats.proportion.proportion_confint(method="wilson")` to 1.1e-16 across 19 cases including the 0/n, n/n and n=1 boundaries; z is pinned to 1.959963984540054 rather than 1.96 (a ~6e-6 difference on the bound) so it is exactly the standard normal 0.975 quantile. | Active |
| D-038 | 2026-08-27 | Methodology §6.2 specifies the hierarchical paired bootstrap in one sentence that is under-determined in four ways, each of which changes the reported interval. Resolved before implementation. (a) **What is paired:** one prompt-ID draw is made per resample and applied to *both* periods, which is what "Baseline and validation are recomputed within the same bootstrap draw" requires; replicate observations are then resampled independently within each period, because replicate 3 of a baseline run and replicate 3 of a validation run are not the same unit and pairing them would be fiction. This is load-bearing and was measured by controlled A/B against the shipped implementation — identical data, identical seed, 10,000 resamples, the *only* difference being whether one prompt draw feeds both periods (`movement.py:199-208`) or each period draws independently. Median 95% CI width at 40 prompts x 4 platforms x 5 replicates: **4.93 AVS points paired vs 15.82 unpaired, a 3.2x inflation**; the ratio holds across scales (2.4x at 12 prompts, 2.9x at 20, 3.3x at 80). On one representative cycle the paired interval is (+4.62, +9.61), which excludes 0 and returns Improvement, while the unpaired interval on the same data is (-1.12, +15.31), which includes 0 and can only return Inconclusive — so the choice decides whether an MRC-5.0 movement is reportable at all. (An earlier uncontrolled estimate put the unpaired width near 26 points; it came from a diagnostic that also varied tier count and the pairing of the underlying data, and is superseded by the A/B figures above.) (b) **Repeated prompts:** a prompt drawn k times in one resample gets k independent replicate resamples, the standard hierarchical treatment. (c) **Empty cells:** a prompt-platform cell holding no scoreable replicate in a given draw is dropped from that PlatformScore's intent-weighted mean and its weight removed from the denominator, rather than imputed as zero — a technical or parsing gap is never scored as an absence (Operating System §1). (d) **Unpaired prompts:** the resampling frame is the intersection of prompts observed in both periods; a prompt present in only one period cannot contribute to a paired delta, is excluded from the frame, and is reported in the result so the exclusion is visible rather than silent. Additionally the reported point-estimate delta is computed from the observed data, not taken as the mean of the bootstrap distribution, and "95% CI excludes 0" is evaluated strictly (a bound of exactly 0.0 includes 0, so the verdict is Inconclusive). Interval calibration was checked by simulation against a known population delta: 98.0% coverage at a nominal 95% over 300 simulated cycles — slightly conservative, the safe direction for a reporting threshold. | Active |
| D-039 | 2026-08-27 | "Observation completeness" — the input to §6.2's Incomplete verdict and to Operating System §11's "cycle completeness below 90% produces Incomplete status" — is defined as scoreable observations divided by planned observations for the run plan, evaluated separately for baseline and validation, with the movement verdict requiring *both* periods to reach 90% and reporting the lower of the two. Reason: the 90% threshold is stated in three documents and its denominator in none, and the choice decides whether a cycle publishes a verdict at all. An observation counts as scoreable only if its status is `complete`, its grounding is not `ungrounded_ineligible` (§8.1 marks those ineligible rather than scoring them), and it carries at least one recommendation row (per D-034 an unparsed observation is incomplete, not absent). The denominator comes from the run plan's own task list, which the planner writes before any provider call (Technical Lane step 3), so it is fixed in advance and cannot shrink to flatter the ratio. Taking the lower of the two periods rather than a pooled figure prevents a well-covered baseline from masking a thin validation run, which is the case the gate exists to catch. | Active |
| D-040 | 2026-08-27 | The score bands in §3.1 (ARS: Fragile 0-39, Developing 40-59, Established 60-74, Strong 75-89, Advanced 90-100) and §4.4 (AVS: Not observed 0-9, Detectable 10-24, Emerging 25-44, Established 45-64, Strong 65-84, Leading 85-100) are printed as integer ranges, but both scores are continuous — ARS is a weighted mean over 70 and AVS is 100 x a mean of means — so values such as 39.5 or 84.7 fall in the gaps between the printed ranges. Bands are therefore evaluated as half-open intervals whose upper edge is the next band's printed lower bound: ARS [0,40) Fragile, [40,60) Developing, [60,75) Established, [75,90) Strong, [90,100] Advanced; AVS [0,10) Not observed, [10,25) Detectable, [25,45) Emerging, [45,65) Established, [65,85) Strong, [85,100] Leading. Chosen over rounding to the nearest integer first, because rounding would lift a 39.5 ARS into Developing and make a client-facing band label depend on a display convention rather than on the score itself. Settled before any report renders a band. | Active |
| D-041 | 2026-08-27 | A positive recommendation at rank 11 or beyond scores RPV 0.00. Reason: Methodology §4.1 tabulates RPV for ranks 1, 2, 3, 4-5 and 6-10 and then simply stops, so a venue recommended in position 11+ has no tabulated value and the lookup had to do *something*. Rejected extrapolating the decay curve past rank 10, which would invent a band the methodology never wrote and put a number in a client report that no document supports. Rejected raising an error, which would let one long list from one provider block an entire cycle's score. 0.00 is the reading most consistent with the table's own trajectory (1.00 -> 0.80 -> 0.65 -> 0.45 -> 0.25, already at the floor of usefulness by rank 6-10) and with §4.1's principle that only meaningful positive recommendations raise AVS. Such a replicate remains `ranked` and scoreable, so it is distinct from `absent` in the evidence trail even though both score 0.00. Revisit if real observations show providers routinely returning lists deep enough for this to bite; the honest fix would be a §4.1 amendment extending the table, not a code change. | Active |
| D-042 | 2026-08-27 | Google AI (Google Search AI Overviews) is structurally evidence-only in v1.0 and can never enter AVS under the current methodology. Reason: §8.4 defines platform eligibility as the *output of a comparison* — "Per platform, API benchmark results are compared with consumer-surface results on recommendation presence and rank behaviour" — so the gate requires both a Layer A leg and a Layer B leg. Google AI has no Layer A leg and cannot acquire one: it is absent from §8.1's provider/adapter table, absent from `observations.provider`'s check constraint, and Google publishes no API for AI Overviews. A third-party SERP scraper does not close the gap, because §8.3 permits automation of a consumer surface only where "a provider explicitly offers an approved automation path for that surface", which a scraper by definition is not. With one leg structurally missing there is no raw mention agreement, no Cohen kappa and no Spearman rho to compute, so Google AI cannot pass — not because it failed the thresholds but because the gate is undefined for it, which is a different and more permanent condition. Explicitly rejected: substituting the Gemini Vertex AI adapter as a proxy benchmark leg for Google AI. Gemini (the assistant surface) and Google Search AI Overviews are different products with different retrieval and different answer construction; scoring one against the other would manufacture an agreement statistic between two things that were never the same measurement, which is precisely the fabrication §8.4 exists to prevent. Consequences, all load-bearing: (a) the maximum eligible-platform count in v1.0 is **four**, not five; (b) Google AI is captured as Layer B consumer-surface evidence, retained, and reported separately per §4.3's "Each platform is also reported separately", but never averaged into AVS; (c) any client-facing statement of AVS coverage must name four platforms, and Google AI's exclusion must be stated rather than left as a silent absence. Revisit only if Google ships a first-party, terms-permitted programmatic surface for AI Overviews — at which point building the Layer A leg and running the gate is a new decision, not an automatic promotion. | Active |
| D-043 | 2026-08-27 | `observations` gains a `surface_layer` column (`'api' \| 'consumer'`, not null, default `'api'`), the `provider` check constraint is widened to admit consumer-layer surfaces including `google_ai`, and `atlas.scoring.loader` admits only `surface_layer = 'api'` into scoring. Migration 0005. Reason: the schema as built could not represent a Layer B capture at all, and the failure was silent in the dangerous direction. Two distinct faults. First, migration 0001 constrains `provider` to `('openai','gemini','perplexity','anthropic')`, so a Google AI consumer capture had no legal value to be written under and the §8.4 calibration could not store half its own input. Second, and worse: for the four platforms that have both an API adapter and a consumer surface, a Layer B capture written with the same `provider` value as its Layer A rows is indistinguishable from an additional replicate of the same platform, so `load_period` would have read human consumer captures as extra API replicates and folded them straight into a client-facing AVS. That inverts both §8.3 (consumer surfaces are human-initiated evidence, not the controlled instrument) and §8.4 (the two layers are the two *sides* of the agreement comparison — averaging them together destroys the comparison it exists to make) in a single query, with no error raised and no visible symptom. The default of `'api'` is chosen so every one of the 26 pre-existing observation rows keeps its current meaning without a data migration, and so an adapter that does not know about this column cannot accidentally write a consumer row. The loader filter is written as a positive allowlist (`== 'api'`) rather than a `!= 'consumer'` exclusion, so any future third layer is excluded from AVS by default and must be admitted deliberately. `evidence.captured_by` already exists from migration 0001 and remains the operator field Operating System §7 requires for human capture; this decision does not change it. | Active |
| D-044 | 2026-08-27 | New append-only `calibration_results` table, keyed by (property, market, platform, calibration run), is the system of record for the §8.4 gate, and `compute_avs` reads its eligible-platform list from that table instead of taking a hand-typed argument. Migration 0005. Reason: D-036 recorded that "no schema column anywhere records per-platform eligibility" and required the caller to name the eligible set to make the act deliberate and auditable. Running the gate now supplies the missing store, and a hand-typed list is no longer the more auditable option — it is the less auditable one, because the list would no longer be a bare assertion but a derivable consequence of stored statistics, and retyping it by hand would let the argument silently diverge from the evidence that justified it. Columns capture the full gate as §8.4 states it, not just its verdict: n paired units, raw mention agreement, Cohen kappa, a prevalence note recording whether kappa was judged stable, co-mentioned observation count, Spearman rho (nullable — §8.4 makes it computable only at >=10 co-mentions), the pass/fail verdict, **which route it passed by** (`kappa` for agreement >=80% plus kappa >=0.60, versus `raw_agreement_manual_review` for the >=85%-plus-documented-review fallback), and the reviewer. Storing the route is load-bearing: the two routes are not equivalent evidence, and a later reader must be able to tell a clean kappa pass from a prevalence-degraded one without re-deriving it. Append-only matches the register's own discipline and Operating System §7's treatment of score-bearing non-personal records — a re-run of calibration writes a new row under a new `calibration_run_id`, and eligibility is read from the latest run, so a superseded result stays visible rather than being overwritten. This narrows D-036 (the eligible set is still explicit and never inferred from the observations present) without retiring it: the requirement that eligibility be a deliberate, recorded act is unchanged; only its storage moves from the caller's keyboard to a table. | Active |
| D-045 | 2026-08-27 | The unit of analysis for every §8.4 agreement statistic is the **prompt-platform cell**, not the replicate. Layer A (n=5, §6.1) and Layer B (n=3 target, §8.3) are each collapsed to one cell-level binary mention judgment by majority vote before raw agreement, Cohen kappa or Spearman rho is computed. Reason: §8.4 states three thresholds and never names their denominator, in the same way §6.2 stated the bootstrap and never named its pairing — the D-038 problem, in a new section. The two readings are not close. Replicate-level pairing has no natural 1:1 correspondence (5 does not pair with 3) and would have to be built as a cross-product, producing N=15 per platform per prompt from what is really one measurement — pseudo-replication that inflates kappa's apparent precision and understates its standard error, so a platform could clear 0.60 on arithmetic that counts the same underlying cell repeatedly. Cell-level pairing gives an honest N of 10 per platform for a 10-prompt Frozen Core, which is small and will frequently fall below §8.4's own >=10 co-mention floor for Spearman — an uncomfortable number, but the true one, and §8.4 already anticipates exactly this case by making rank agreement descriptive rather than rescuing below that floor. §8.4's own wording ("at least 10 co-mentioned **observations**") reads as the cell, not the replicate. Settled before any statistic was computed, so the threshold is not chosen after seeing which reading passes. Tie handling: both replicate counts are odd by design, so majority does not tie in the intended case; if an operational shortfall (a failed call, an abandoned capture) drops a cell to an even count, the tie resolves to `absent` and the cell is logged for manual review — never silently broken toward mention, which would flatter agreement, and never silently dropped, which would shrink the denominator invisibly. A cell with no usable replicate on either side is excluded from that platform's frame and reported, per the D-038(d) precedent that an exclusion must be visible rather than silent. Cohen kappa and Spearman rho are hand-implemented on the standard library following D-037's no-numpy/no-scipy precedent, each verified against a reference implementation to a stated tolerance recorded in the test suite; Spearman uses midrank tie correction, which is what `scipy.stats.spearmanr` computes and what co-mentioned rank data with repeated positions requires. | Active |
| D-046 | 2026-08-27 | A Frozen Core prompt flagged non-diagnostic under §7 (returning the client in >90% of baseline replicates across every eligible platform) **stays in the frozen set with no rebaseline**, is excluded from the AVS intent-weighted mean, and is reported separately as an entity-accuracy check. The exclusion is conditional on the flag actually tripping against baseline data — it is never written into the prompt set as an unconditional property of a prompt. Reason: §7 says such a prompt "is flagged non-diagnostic" and then stops, defining no consequence. That would be deferrable except that §7 also makes set membership immutable — "Prompt changes that alter intent, entity, tier or set membership require a major prompt-set version and a new baseline" — so discovering the flag at baseline and reacting by removing the prompt would invalidate the entire calibration run that discovered it. The rule therefore has to exist before the set is frozen, not after the data arrives. Retention-with-exclusion is the only reading that satisfies both halves of §7 at once: membership is untouched, so no rebaseline is triggered, while a prompt carrying no discriminating signal stops contributing to a score that is supposed to measure discrimination. Excluding the cell from the intent-weighted mean and removing its weight from the denominator reuses the D-038(c) mechanism already built for empty cells, so no new scoring path is introduced. The separate report is not a consolation: a saturated branded prompt is the instrument that surfaces `entity_conflict` — wrong-entity and name-collision behaviour, which §4.1 routes to P3 rather than AVS — so the prompt keeps doing the job it is actually good at. Conditionality is the load-bearing half of this decision. Writing "excluded from AVS" into a prompt's definition at authoring time would prejudge an empirical result, would make the AVS denominator depend on an author's expectation rather than on observed replicate behaviour, and would silently change the score if a prompt that was expected to saturate turned out not to. The flag is computed from baseline observations, stored, and only then does it exclude. A prompt that stops saturating in a later cycle stops being excluded, and the change is visible in the stored flag rather than requiring a prompt-set edit. | Active |
| D-047 | 2026-08-27 | Rank collapse for a co-mentioned prompt-platform cell is the **median rank across that cell's mentioned replicates**, computed per layer, on the same cells D-045 collapses for mention agreement. Reason: D-045 fixed the unit of analysis as the cell and specified the *mention* collapse (majority vote) but not the rank collapse, and Spearman rho needs one rank value per cell per layer — majority vote does not produce one. The gap was found while implementing D-045, not in review, and it changes a reported §8.4 statistic, so it is settled here rather than left to the code. Median is chosen because rank is ordinal: a position is a place in a list, not a quantity, so an arithmetic mean over positions asserts a spacing the data does not carry (the distance from rank 1 to rank 2 is not the same kind of thing as the distance from rank 9 to rank 10, and §4.1's own RPV table prices those gaps very differently — 1.00 -> 0.80 versus 0.25 -> 0.25). Median is additionally robust to a single outlying replicate, which matters at n=5 and n=3 where one anomalous deep placement would drag a mean several positions and misrepresent a cell that was otherwise stable. The choice is deliberately low-stakes by construction: the collapsed ranks feed only Spearman, which re-ranks its inputs before correlating, so **only relative ordering between cells survives the collapse** — any monotone-preserving summary of a cell's replicates would give the same rho, and median is the one that is also a real observed position in the odd-count case. Rejected: the mean (imposes interval spacing on ordinal data); the best (minimum) rank (systematically flatters the client by reporting its luckiest replicate); the modal rank (undefined or arbitrary whenever replicates disagree, which is the normal case). Even-count ties resolve as `statistics.median` does, by averaging the two middle positions — a non-integer cell rank is acceptable precisely because only ordering is used downstream, and no client-facing number reports a cell rank directly. Replicates that were not mentions contribute no rank and are excluded from the median rather than entering it as a sentinel; a cell whose majority judgment is absent carries no rank at all. This decision changes how a cell's rank *value* is derived and nothing about the statistics themselves — the kappa and Spearman implementations, and their verification against sklearn 1.9.0 and scipy 1.18.1 recorded under D-045, are untouched. | Active |
| D-048 | 2026-08-27 | `recommendations` gains a unique constraint on (`observation_id`, `entity_name`). Migration 0006; no application change, because the only writer already refuses the import that would violate it. Reason: `atlas.tools.rpv_labeling` validates an import by reading which observation ids already have recommendation rows and rejecting any row that names one, so duplicates cannot arise from one human running one deliberate import. That is a check-then-act guard, not a constraint: it reads the existing set once and inserts afterwards, so two imports overlapping in time (a second labeler, a re-run started before the first finished, a scripted backfill) both validate against the same pre-insert state and both write. The guard is therefore correct today only because of an operational fact — one operator, one import at a time — and the standing test in this register is whether a rule survives the process changing rather than whether it holds under today's staffing. Labeling stops being single-operator at the point labeling volume exceeds one person, which is Client #1, not some distant scale milestone. What makes it worth a constraint rather than a note is that the failure is silent: for the client entity the scoring loader already raises loudly on a second client-entity row per observation (D-034), but competitor rows are checked nowhere, and a duplicated competitor quietly double-counts that entity in §9 Share of Voice — a wrong number with no error, which is the defect class this register exists to catch before a client report. Scope of the key is one row per entity per observation, so a response listing the same entity twice is now rejected at write time instead of yielding two RPV values for one entity; the labeling rule in that case is a single row at the entity's best (lowest) rank, which is the only reading consistent with §4.1 RPV being a position value held by an entity in an observation. Not covered, and flagged rather than assumed away: rows with a null `observation_id`, since Postgres treats NULLs as distinct under a unique constraint — acceptable because such a row is already outside every scoring path, all of which join through `observation_id`. No backfill: no duplicate pair exists today, and if one somehow did the migration fails on apply rather than discarding a row, which is the correct outcome — a duplicate is resolved by a human deciding which row is right. | Active |
| D-049 | 2026-08-27 | `evidence` gains `prompt_version`, `provider`, `model`, `tool_version`, `market`, `language` and `source_reference` (migration 0007), and `atlas.evidence.vault.store_evidence` writes them. The full §7 set continues to be written to the Drive file's appProperties as well. Reason: Operating System §7 requires that "every evidence record carries evidence ID, run ID, prompt version, provider/model/tool version, market, language, UTC timestamp, source reference, payload hash and operator where human capture is used". The table from migration 0001 holds only observation id, run id, payload hash, manifest id, storage path, data class, captured_by and captured_at; the seven fields above had no column anywhere in the database and travelled on Drive appProperties alone — the gap named in `drive.EVIDENCE_TABLE_GAP` during Technical Lane step 8, recorded there as needing a migration and a decision. This is that decision, and it closes the gap. Drive-only is the wrong system of record for these fields on three counts. They are not queryable: "which observations were captured under prompt set v1.0 against gpt-5.6 in TH" is an ordinary audit and reproducibility question, and answering it from Drive means listing files and parsing per-file metadata instead of one SQL predicate. They are stored lossily: Drive caps an appProperty at 124 bytes of key plus value and the uploader truncates rather than failing the upload, which is right for a self-describing artifact and wrong for the authoritative copy of a `source_reference`. And they sit behind a different access boundary from the row that cites them, so provenance would depend on a Drive service account staying reachable and on file metadata never being edited outside this code path. Keeping the Drive copy is deliberate rather than redundancy for its own sake: an artifact that describes itself is what stays intelligible to an auditor holding the file without the database, and it is the only copy that survives the row and the file being separated. So the database copy is the queryable one and the one an audit is answered from; the Drive copy is the artifact's self-description, and neither is dropped. Columns are nullable: the table is empty so no backfill arises, but `tool_version` and `source_reference` are legitimately absent for some captures (a call with no tool leg; a capture with no consumer URL), and a not-null column would force a placeholder that makes an absent value indistinguishable from an empty one. No check constraint on `evidence.provider`, unlike `observations.provider`: evidence covers data classes that have no AI provider at all (`public_review_text`), so the platform vocabulary is not a valid constraint on this table — free text here matches `costs.provider` and is intentional, not an oversight. | Active |
| D-050 | 2026-08-27 | Amends **D-048**. D-048's clause "Migration 0006; no application change, because the only writer already refuses the import that would violate it" is superseded: the constraint did require an application change, and it has been made. Everything else in D-048 — the constraint itself, migration 0006, the key's scope, the null-`observation_id` carve-out and the fail-on-apply rule — is unaffected and stands as written. Reason: the premise was checked against the code after D-048 was accepted and is false in one case. `atlas.tools.rpv_labeling.validate` rejects an observation that already has recommendation rows in the database, and rejects a second client-entity row for one observation (D-034), but it never compared (`observation_id`, `entity_name`) between rows of the SAME sheet. Verified against the real validator, not read off the source: three rows for one observation with the same competitor at rank 2 and rank 7 returned ok=True. Post-0006 that import reaches Postgres and dies on `recommendations_observation_id_entity_name_key`. Nothing is partially written — `import_from_sheet` inserts the batch in one statement, so the atomicity D-048 relied on does hold — but the labeler is handed a constraint name instead of a sheet row number, which contradicts the rule the tool is built on: a wrong label is rejected WITH ITS SHEET ROW, never quietly repaired. A constraint that can only be explained by a Postgres error message is a worse guard than the application check it was meant to outlast. The change: `validate` now tracks each (`observation_id`, `entity_name`) pair to the sheet row that first claimed it and emits a `RowError` on the repeat, naming the earlier row, in the same shape as the D-034 duplicate-client-row error. It runs after the client-entity check so a duplicated CLIENT row still gets D-034's more specific message. It matches on the raw strings with no case or whitespace normalisation, deliberately: a stricter check would reject imports the database would accept, which is the same class of drift between tool and schema that this amendment exists to correct, only pointing the other way. The general point, worth keeping: "no application change needed" is a claim about what an existing guard actually covers, and it has to be verified against that guard rather than inferred from the guard existing. D-048's status stays Active — this amends one clause, it does not retire the decision. | Active |
| D-051 | 2026-08-28 | Frozen Core competitor set for Samujana (§8.4 calibration) locked to: Kerem Villas, Sukkho Samui Estates, Magic Suites – Samui Luxury Private Pool Villas, Sandalwood Luxury Villas, Horizon Villas Samui. Replaces draft set (Six Senses, Cape Fahn, Conrad, W, Four Seasons), category-inappropriate for Villa/Estate profile. Tier A–D structure per §4.2 unchanged; only named-competitor prompts affected. | Active |
| D-052 | 2026-08-28 | Samujana GBP URL confirmed: https://maps.google.com/?cid=11853452962372942458. Unblocks scripts/seed_calibration_property.py --commit per §8.4 property-selection criteria. | Active |
| D-053 | 2026-08-28 | §8.4 calibration property-selection criteria review_presence and third_party_reference were absent from schema — only website_url and google_business_profile_url existed, leaving 2 of 4 §8.4 criteria unverifiable in code. Added as verification pointers on properties: review_presence_verified/review_presence_evidence_ref and third_party_reference_verified/third_party_reference_evidence_ref, nullable until populated, evidence stored via the existing vault.store_evidence() hash-verified pipeline (D-049 pattern). Enables atlas/calibration/gate.py to check all four §8.4 criteria programmatically. | Active |
| D-054 | 2026-08-28 | Client entity never named in competitor-naming Frozen Core prompts — operational extension of §7's "client never in its own competitor set" to prompt phrasing, not just result sets. Applies to all future Frozen Core prompt sets, not only Samujana's. | Active |
| D-055 | 2026-08-28 | §7's 90%-non-diagnostic flag scoped to Tiers A–C only. Tier D is branded/navigational by design and expected to return the client near-100% of baseline replicates; exempt by definition, not a gate failure. | Active |
| D-056 | 2026-08-28 | Calibration run driver (calibration-run-driver-DESIGN.md) scoped to Layer A only, one calibration property, one finalized prompt set, one-shot invocation; reuses the existing run_plans row for a given property + prompt-set-version rather than creating duplicates. Layer B (consumer-surface) capture has no run_plans creation path in the codebase and is explicitly out of scope for this driver. Ownership and timeline for building that path is unresolved and must be assigned before atlas.calibration.gate.run_gate can run end-to-end, since the gate requires consumer_run_plan_ids alongside api_run_plan_ids. | Active |
| D-057 | 2026-08-28 | §8.4's "review presence" and "public third-party references" property-selection criteria were unquantified in the methodology (the only unquantified bullet in §8.4, verified by exhaustive grep — no other section defines them; §5.3/§5.4 are scoring pillars, not eligibility criteria, and are explicitly not borrowed here). Minimal bar set for calibration-property eligibility: review presence = a live listing with visible guest reviews on at least one major platform (e.g. Google, TripAdvisor). Public third-party reference = at least one instance of independent third-party coverage or listing not published by the property itself (press mention, travel publication, curated list, etc.). Evidence stored per D-053/migration 0008. | Active |
| D-058 | 2026-08-28 | §6.1's minimum six-hour run window for Frozen Core replicates has no stated rationale in the methodology text. Given the window appears in the same sentence as "fresh sessions" and "no conversation history" (both independence-preserving conditions), and Frozen Core baseline is the only run type in §6.1's table that may support a movement verdict, the window is treated as a binding measurement condition for the calibration baseline, not advisory documentation. The execution mechanism to satisfy it (manual spacing across multiple run plans, vs. other approaches) is a separate, not-yet-resolved question, since resume_run has no pacing and drains a committed run plan's full queue in one pass regardless of invocation timing. **Addendum:** Splitting the baseline into multiple run plans to satisfy §6.1's window was investigated. run_gate pools by (prompt_version_id, provider) and is indifferent to plan count, so a split would satisfy the gate. However, compute_avs_for_property takes a single run_plan_id and would silently under-report against a partial plan, reading 100% completeness while holding only a fraction of the intended replicates — a correctness risk worse than not splitting. Splitting also requires new_plan=True on every commit beyond the first, repurposing that flag away from its documented "deliberate second baseline" meaning. Given AVS's single-run_plan_id assumption is a shared, tested component and changing it is out of scope for this baseline, the Samujana Frozen Core baseline will run as a single run_plan_id, not spaced across six hours. This is a known, accepted deviation from §6.1's stated minimum window for this baseline only, pending P1-02 (scheduling infrastructure) or a future decision revisiting the window's actual purpose before it becomes a recurring validation cycle. | Active |
| D-059 | 2026-08-29 | Each Layer B replicate is captured in a **fresh incognito session** — logged out, window fully closed between replicates, no account signed in on any surface, VPN off or set consistently to the client's market. Reason: §8.3 states "target n=3" and never names what the count is *of* — a D-045-shaped gap, since D-045 fixed the unit of analysis for agreement statistics but not the independence of the replicates feeding it. Back-to-back replicates in one signed-in or persistent-session browser window share conversation context and personalisation, so `collapse_replicates`'s majority vote would be collapsing one measurement and two echoes of it, not three independent draws — exactly the pseudo-replication D-045 exists to prevent, now at the capture stage rather than the analysis stage. Rejected: signed-in capture, which would certify the API against the operator's own account history (not the client's target consumer), cannot be re-run identically for a later quarterly comparison since account history keeps accumulating (§9 reproducibility requires a repeatable protocol, not a moving one), and is indefensible to a client as representative of "what a consumer sees." Accepted limitation, stated explicitly rather than left implicit: Layer B under this protocol is a **controlled** consumer surface, not a naturalistic one — no real user browses logged out with a closed session between every query. Personalisation effects on recommendation behaviour are a real and separate question; this decision keeps them out of calibration rather than smuggling an unmeasured confound into an agreement statistic. Consequence: the calibration capture campaign spans multiple sessions across days, not one sitting, which is also why D-061 (below) matters. **Addendum (2026-08-31):** D-066 replaces this decision's VPN mechanism with an IPRoyal residential proxy across all four consumer surfaces — read "VPN" above as superseded tooling under the same independence intent, not a live requirement. D-067 documents a scoped, surface-specific exception to "no account signed in on any surface" for claude-web only, where no anonymous access path exists; the other three surfaces remain governed by this decision as written. | Active |
| D-060 | 2026-08-29 | Consumer-surface `model` values are governed literals, one per surface, each meaning "this consumer surface as presented on the capture date": `'perplexity-web'`, `'chatgpt-web'`, `'gemini-web'`, `'claude-web'`, `'google-ai-overviews'`. Reason: `observations.model` is NOT NULL with a `''` default, and a consumer web UI exposes no queryable model identifier the way an API response does — there is no version string to record faithfully. Writing `''` is honest but discards surface identity entirely (which of five consumer surfaces produced this row becomes unrecoverable from `model` alone); fabricating a version-shaped string (e.g. inventing a build number) would assert a precision the capture cannot support and could be mistaken for an API-sourced value on later inspection. A documented literal is deliberately coarse — it names the surface, not a version — matching what a human capture can actually attest to. These values were already in use in the ingest tool with no decision behind them; this entry is that decision. Revisit if a consumer surface begins exposing a stable, checkable version identifier, at which point that surface's literal can be replaced without touching the others. | Active |
| D-061 | 2026-08-29 | §6.1's minimum six-hour run window does not bind Layer B capture. Distinct from D-058, which accepted a deviation from that same window for an *API* run (Layer A baseline, single `run_plan_id`, `resume_run` draining a committed queue with no pacing). §6.1 governs the API instrument specifically — its rationale sits alongside "fresh sessions" and "no conversation history," conditions that describe automated replicate spacing within one controlled instrument. A human capture campaign is a different kind of thing: D-059 already requires a fresh incognito session per replicate, which is a stronger independence guarantee than a time window achieves indirectly, and the campaign is expected to span days by design, not six hours, simply because it depends on an operator's availability across multiple sessions. Registering this separately from D-058 rather than folding it in keeps the two deviations distinguishable — D-058 is an accepted gap against an instrument the window was written for; this is a statement that the window was never the applicable rule to begin with. | Active |
| D-062 | 2026-08-30 | Layer B run_plans rows have nullable window_start/window_end (migration 0001) but no defined semantics for when they're set, unlike Layer A where plan_calibration_run computes a forward-looking window at creation via window_hours. Consumer-surface capture is retrospective — the real window isn't known until all replicates are captured — so a placeholder value at creation risks silent staleness if the post-capture update is missed, same failure class as D-057's evidence-write idempotency gap. Decision: insert with window_start/window_end = null and status='planned'; update both fields to the actual first-replicate-start/last-replicate-end timestamps once all 3 replicates are captured, alongside the status transition into the ingest-ready state. Rejected: setting a planned/placeholder window at creation and correcting it post-capture — indistinguishable from a real value if the correction step is skipped, and null already carries "unset" semantics elsewhere in this schema (migration 0008). | Active |
| D-063 | 2026-08-30 | run_plans.status has three legal-but-unused enum values ('queued', 'running', 'complete') per migration 0001's check constraint. No code reads or gates on run_plans.status anywhere in the ingest path (consumer_ingest.py:548 selects only id, replicate_count); the only two writers are driver.py ('planned' at insert) and reconcile.py ('reconciled', unconditional, post-check). Decision: Layer B run_plans creation writes 'planned' at insert (consistent with Layer A) and updates to 'complete' once all 3 replicates are captured and window_start/window_end are set per D-062, immediately preceding reconcile.py's 'reconciled' write — matching Layer A's positional sequencing even though no consumer currently checks the intermediate value. This is an inference from existing schema vocabulary, not a verified contract; if a future consumer needs to gate on an in-progress state, 'queued'/'running' remain available and unclaimed. | Active |
| D-064 | 2026-08-30 | §8 item 5 — Layer B capture automation posture. Full automation of consumer-surface capture was considered and rejected: D-042 excludes Google AI Overviews from the frozen-core measurement specifically because it cannot be captured without automation-adjacent tooling that risks triggering different serving behavior than an ordinary logged-out user session — automating capture broadly would undermine the same premise for the surfaces currently included. Decision: manual capture retained as the default operational model; assisted-capture tooling (session setup, prompt-sequencing helpers, screenshot/timestamp logging) is permitted since it doesn't alter what is served, only how the operator's actions are recorded. Any future move toward automated response retrieval is a §10 change-control matter, not a tooling upgrade — it changes what D-059's "controlled surface" claim means. **Addendum (2026-08-31):** D-067 documents a further scoped deviation from the controlled-surface posture referenced above — claude-web has no anonymous access path and is captured via one dedicated, single-purpose logged-in account with Incognito chat mode, not full logged-out anonymity. This is an account-level anonymity substitute for that one surface, not a change to the automation posture itself. | Active |
| D-065 | 2026-08-30 | §8 item 4 — task_id synthesis and upsert idempotency for human-originated observations. task_id_for() uses deterministic plain string interpolation (consumer:{run_plan_id}:{provider}:{prompt_version_id}:{replicate_index}) rather than a hash, unlike planner.deterministic_task_id. Rejected alternative: hashing for consistency with the Layer A planner — rejected because the stated purpose (an operator reconciling a capture sheet against the database needs to read the id and identify the cell) requires the tuple to stay legible, and uniqueness is already guaranteed by the tuple itself being unique per cell, so hashing would add opacity with no idempotency benefit. _write_observation upserts on task_id and hard-fails (raises) rather than silently proceeding if the upsert returns no row — this guards the evidence write that follows. _store_capture_evidence always supplies a real, non-null observation_id (from _write_observation's return, the row's own PK, not task_id), so vault.store_evidence()'s on_conflict="observation_id" guard is live for this path — unlike store_84_evidence.py's property-level evidence, which has no observation_id to key on and must guard on payload_hash instead (see D-057 evidence-collection notes). Conclusion: no gap exists in the consumer-ingest observation/evidence write path; idempotency is sound by construction because observation-before-evidence ordering guarantees a real key exists before the evidence upsert runs. | Active |
| D-066 | 2026-08-31 | Residential proxy (IPRoyal) replaces shared VPN for all four `CONSUMER_SURFACE_MODELS` captures (`chatgpt-web`, `gemini-web`, `perplexity-web`, `claude-web`; `google-ai-overviews` is out of scope — D-042 keeps it evidence-only with no Layer B capture and no VPN/proxy dependency). Reason: shared/datacenter VPN IPs (NordVPN, two distinct UK exit nodes tested) triggered a categorical pre-answer login gate on perplexity-web. Confirmed not IP-specific (two different servers, same result) and not a general Perplexity policy change (a no-VPN control passed clean). Root cause: Perplexity's stated network-authenticity checks flag datacenter/proxy-range IPs regardless of shared-vs-dedicated status. Decision: retire VPN in favor of an IPRoyal residential proxy across all four surfaces rather than Perplexity alone, for setup consistency and because the untested risk on the other three surfaces is the same class of risk, not a different one. Country is set via the `_country-xx` password parameter (ISO 3166-1 alpha-2) against a single purchased GB pool, not a separate proxy purchase per market — intended to support a future per-market capture rollout, though the general multi-market policy decision that would govern that rollout has not itself been drafted or logged under any ID (see Open items, below). Session TTL is set to 4h (`lifetime-4h`) to comfortably cover one full replicate without mid-run re-authentication. Config: `geo.iproyal.com:12321`, HTTP protocol, Basic auth via Edge's `--proxy-server` flag plus a dedicated `--user-data-dir`, InPrivate; credentials are entered per-session via the browser auth prompt, never embedded in the launch command. Operational note for future sessions: initial setup hit persistent 403s across three credential regenerations; root cause was a domain typo in IPRoyal's own dashboard cURL example (`iconhazip.com` instead of the real `icanhazip.com`), not a credentials or account fault — verify the target domain independently before assuming a credential failure. | Active |
| D-067 | 2026-08-31 | claude-web has no anonymous access path; captured under a scoped logged-in exception with Incognito chat mode. Reason: unlike perplexity-web, chatgpt-web and gemini-web (all of which support genuine anonymous querying), claude.ai requires account sign-in before any prompt can be sent — confirmed via both direct navigation and the "Try Claude" entry point, with no guest/anonymous path found on either. Decision: capture proceeds via one dedicated, single-purpose free-tier account (not tied to personal or client identity, used for nothing else), with Incognito chat mode enabled for every capture prompt; Incognito was verified live, before this decision was logged, to start each conversation with no memory pulled in and to create no new memory or history entry. Documented deviation: this breaks the logged-out-throughout protocol (D-059, and D-064's restatement of D-059's controlled-surface claim) for claude-web specifically. It is the closest available substitute for an anonymous first-time user given the product's architecture, not full equivalence — Anthropic still receives and retains the conversation under standard account-level retention (30 days default), and the account carries an identity even though individual chats do not carry memory. This is an account-level anonymity substitute, not the request-level anonymity the other three surfaces achieve. Scope: applies to claude-web only; the other three surfaces remain fully logged out, no account. This asymmetry must be flagged explicitly in any client-facing methodology writeup rather than presenting all four surfaces as equivalently anonymous. | Active |
| D-068 | 2026-09-01 | Perplexity Layer A migration from Sonar Chat Completions (D-031) to the Agent API is governed by a measurement-contract equivalence gate, not an integration test. The Agent adapter is built immediately behind the provider abstraction, but production cutover requires all of: (a) the resolved model identity is Perplexity-native or explicitly pinned, stable, and recordable in evidence per observation; (b) web/search invocation is verifiable from the response, not inferred; (c) citations and search results map deterministically into the Evidence Vault; (d) repeated behaviour on the Frozen Core prompt set is demonstrated comparable with the current Sonar baseline (side-by-side run, documented). Reason: Atlas measures Perplexity — an endpoint swap that resolves to a third-party routed model changes the instrument even when the migration technically succeeds, invalidating cross-period comparability. Deadline branch: if equivalence is not demonstrated by 2026-09-20, a further decision explicitly sets Perplexity Layer A's status for the Samujana calibration window (evidence-only, or excluded from the §8.4 gate) — Sonar is never silently run past the retirement date and the migration is never silently deferred, per D-031's own instruction that the deadline must not lapse silently. The 2026-09-27 retirement date is revalidated against an authoritative Perplexity source and downgraded from "confirmed shutdown" to "internal migration deadline" if unverifiable; the internal deadline is retained either way. | Active |
| D-069 | 2026-09-01 | The conflicting factual records on the perplexity-web anonymous signup wall (D-066-era observations vs. the later roadblock brief) are resolved by a controlled matrix, not a spot check. Conditions: {direct residential connection, IPRoyal residential proxy} × 3 clean sessions each, identical prompt, browser, incognito/session and account state per D-059, executed within one 24-hour window. Recorded per session: UTC timestamp, IP geography and type, account state, browser/session state, wall outcome (gated / not gated), screenshot evidence per Layer B requirements. Outcome rules: wall reproduces in all six → perplexity-web has no anonymous access path; a D-067-pattern scoped logged-in profile (dedicated Atlas test account, memory off) is created as a new surface-profile version. Wall absent on direct but present via proxy → IP-reputation artifact; anonymous capture stands, proxy usage for perplexity-web is re-decided. Mixed/intermittent → anonymous access is treated as unstable and therefore not a reproducible protocol; the logged-in profile path is taken. The resulting decision explicitly names and supersedes whichever prior factual record the matrix disproves. | Active |
## Open items

- **Samujana capture geography + general multi-market policy — never drafted.** Commit `fca9e02` ("Register D-066, D-067: Samujana capture geography + general multi-market policy") does not match its own diff: it added D-062–D-065 (Layer B run_plans semantics, status field, automation posture, task_id idempotency), none of which concern capture geography or multi-market policy. A full-history search (2026-08-31) across every commit and branch found no content matching either topic under any ID. These decisions remain undrafted and unlogged; D-066 and D-067 above are unrelated decisions (residential proxy, claude-web exception) that took the next available sequential IDs. Log the capture-geography and multi-market-policy decisions under the next available ID(s) once drafted.

## Adding a decision

1. Determine version treatment per Methodology §10 (patch/clarification vs.
   new baseline vs. major methodology version vs. operating update).
2. Add a row here with the next sequential D-xxx ID, dated, with the
   decision stated in full — not just a reference to a discussion.
3. Only then implement it in the methodology/operating docs and/or code.
4. If the decision retires an earlier one, mark the earlier row's Status
   column "Retired by D-xxx" — do not delete or rewrite it.
